          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The speaker explicitly presents loving affection as true, qualified as sisterly rather than romantic or marital love. This avowal is the focal expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not itself undertake marriage, travel, service, or another future obligation. Its sisterly qualification occurs while the speaker rejects being his wife; separate offers concerning India must not be transferred onto the love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I will be your curate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression appears within a deliberate explanation of the speaker’s reasons and relationship to St. John, not as a reflex-like or affectively discharged verbal response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The core force is adequately represented as a qualified avowal of affection. Although it also helps explain why she would have risked much with him, no distinct residual force beyond T is central to the expression itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is disputing St. John’s claim that she is obligated to go to India. She explains that she would have risked much with him because she admires, trusts, and loves him as a sister, while refusing both marriage and an unconditional obligation to go.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal love expression makes no undertaking. Separately, Jane offers to accompany St. John as an assistant or curate but refuses to become his wife. St. John rejects the proposed arrangement of an unmarried female curate accompanying him and suggests possible service with a married missionary instead; Jane denies being obligated to accept that alternative.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognisable as being from Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre; the classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports a sisterly avowal and provides narrator-level signs of concern, but it cannot by itself conclusively establish the full history, durability, or precise depth of Jane’s affection.\"}"
